\section{Why this exists, and why it is not in the test suite}

A thank-you page is a promise.  When the server redirects a student to it, the
answer has to be there afterwards: in the answer log, on the results page, in
[[mentipy export]] and in [[mentipy view]].  The store and the server go a long
way to keep that promise while everything around them misbehaves, and the
chapter on the store explains how: answers are appended to a log that nothing
rewrites, so neither a build rewriting the store, nor an older Mentipy that
ignores the lock, nor a build stopped while it holds that lock, can undo one.

Unit tests can check each of those mechanisms, but they cannot check the claim
itself, because the claim is about real processes meeting at moments nobody
chose.  So this program does what the lecture does.  It starts the real
[[mentipy serve]] on a real disk, sends real HTTP requests at the rate of a class
answering, runs real builds against the same store, and sends real signals.
Afterwards it asks every reader for every answer it was thanked for.

It stays out of [[make test]] and out of a plain [[pytest]] for three reasons.
It adds five minutes to a suite that is already slow.  It needs the network, for a
real Mentipy 0.17 from PyPI.  And its result depends on timing, so it says
something different from a unit test: a failure is always real, while a pass only
says that the race did not happen in these seconds on this machine.  Run it after
a change to how answers or the store are written, and before a release.

Nothing has to be remembered to keep it out of the suite.  The file is not named
[[test_*.py]], so [[pytest]] does not collect it; it lies outside [[src/]] and has
no test chunk, so the auto-discovery in [[tests/Makefile]] does not see it; and the
wheel packages only [[src/mentipy]].  It is not part of the manual
either, which is what we hand to lecturers.  [[make -C tests/stress race.pdf]]
typesets this document on its own.

\subsection{What counts as lost}

An answer is \emph{accepted} when the server has replied with the redirect to the
thank-you page, status 303.  An accepted answer is \emph{lost} when a reader
cannot find it after the run.  Each open answer carries a number of its own, so
for those the program looks for the very answer.  The multiple-choice answers all
say the same thing, so there it can only compare the counts, and an unanswered
request that was stored anyway could hide a lost answer in the count.  That is why
the three questions are two open ones and only one multiple-choice one.

Two other outcomes are not losses, because the student can tell that something
went wrong.
\begin{description}
\item[Refused] The server replied with anything but 303: the student sees an error
  page and answers again.
\item[No reply] The connection failed, typically because we had just killed the
  server.  Such an answer may or may not have been stored, and the student has
  not been thanked either way.
\end{description}
They are only acceptable where the scenario itself causes them, and elsewhere they
fail the run as well (\cref{sec:verdict}).

\subsection{The whole program}

[[main]] runs one scenario after another and prints a table.  Each scenario gets a
[[Run]] in a directory of its own, and the five parts of a run are the sections
that follow.
\begin{description}
\item[Servers] (\cref{sec:servers}) One or two real [[mentipy serve]] processes on
  a store with three questions.
\item[Respondents] (\cref{sec:respondents}) Threads that answer at the rate of a
  class and note what each answer got as a reply, while another thread keeps
  reading the pages.
\item[The scenario] (\cref{sec:scenarios}) What goes wrong meanwhile, in the main
  thread, until the time is up.
\item[The readers] (\cref{sec:readers}) With the respondents stopped and the server
  still up, every reader is asked for every accepted answer.
\item[The verdict] (\cref{sec:verdict}) Lost answers and anything else that should
  not have happened.
\end{description}

