Stopping is where a run keeps the machine clean, whatever happened before.  The
servers get the same SIGINT as a lecturer's Ctrl-C and a chance to finish, which is
also a check that they do.  After that every process this run started and has not
reaped gets a SIGKILL, which also works on a process that is stopped.  The harness
only ever signals the processes it started itself, since other people's servers
may be running on the same machine.

<<run methods>>=
def stop(self):
    """Leave no process of this run behind, whatever has happened."""

    self.stopping.set()
    for server in self.servers:
        self._stop_server(server)
    for child in self.children:
        if child.poll() is None:
            child.kill()
        child.wait()
    if self.server_log is not None:
        self.server_log.close()
<<the state of a run>>=
self.server_log = None
