\subsection{Builds, with and without the lock}

The ordinary case is a lecturer who recompiles while the class answers.  Both take
turns on the store through the lock, and the answers do not even touch the store.

<<functions>>=
def drive_builds(run):
    """Build in a loop, which is also what `two-servers` does."""

    while run.running():
        run.build()
@

An older Mentipy in a deck's environment is the case the lock cannot help with,
since 0.17 rewrites the store without knowing of the answer log.  It has to be the
real package: a stand-in written today would embody what we believe 0.17 does.  So
that the scenario cannot pass without having done its job, it looks for the mark the
old writer leaves, the [[answers]] key that it puts in the store and that no current
writer adds.

<<functions>>=
def drive_old_builds(run):
    """Build in a loop with the old Mentipy."""

    while run.running():
        run.build(old=True)
    if "answers" not in json.loads(run.store.read_text()):
        run.problems.append(
            f"the store has no answers key, so no Mentipy {OLD_MENTIPY} wrote it"
        )
@

A killed build, the lecturer's Ctrl-C or a crash, can die in the middle of writing a
temporary file or while holding the lock.  The lock goes with the process and the
file is only ever moved into place whole, so the server should see nothing.

<<functions>>=
def drive_kill_builds(run):
    """Kill builds at random moments."""

    while run.running():
        run.build(kill_after=random.uniform(0.2, 1.0))
@

A stopped build is worse than a killed one, because it keeps the lock: a laptop
closed during a compile, or Ctrl-Z in a terminal.  We freeze a real build from
outside at a random moment, run another build meanwhile, which has to wait for the
lock and then go ahead without it, and then let the frozen one continue from a store
that has changed behind its back.  The notes show the number of questions before,
during and after, so a reader can see that the second build got through.

<<functions>>=
def drive_stop_build(run):
    """Freeze a build with SIGSTOP while another one runs."""

    while run.running():
        frozen = run.start_build(fillers=60)
        time.sleep(random.uniform(0.3, 1.2))
        frozen.send_signal(signal.SIGSTOP)
        before = run.question_count()
        run.build(fillers=5)
        during = run.question_count()
        time.sleep(random.uniform(0.2, 1.0))
        frozen.send_signal(signal.SIGCONT)
        run.finish_build(frozen)
        after = run.question_count()
        run.notes.append(
            f"questions before={before} during={during} after={after}"
        )
@

\subsection{Faults in the server}

Two servers on one store is what happens when a lecturer starts a second one on
another port.  Both append to the same log, which only works because each line is a
single [[O_APPEND]] write.  It runs the driver of [[builds]] and differs only in the
table at the end of this section.

A killed server is the promise itself: everything it thanked for must have left the
process by then.  This cannot test the sync behind that promise, since the page cache
survives a killed process; only a pulled plug would show that, and nothing here pulls
one.  What it does show is that no answer waits in a buffer in the process, and that
a new server reads everything the old one wrote.

<<functions>>=
def drive_kill_server(run):
    """SIGKILL the server at random moments and start it again."""

    restarts = 0
    while run.running():
        time.sleep(random.uniform(1, 3))
        run.restart_server(0, kill=True)
        restarts += 1
    run.notes.append(f"the server was SIGKILLed {restarts} times")
@

A full disk is the fault that gives a short write: part of a line, and an error.  A
limit on the size of the server's files gives the same with no harm to the machine,
as the system cuts the write off at the limit and refuses the next one.  A server
that meets it must refuse the answers from then on, and may leave half a line, which
the next server has to step over.  We set the limit so that the log reaches it about a
quarter of the way through, and restart without it half-way, so that answers
accepted before, and after, must all be there at the end.  A run that never reached
the limit has tested nothing, so that is a problem too; it takes a very short
[[--seconds]] to get there.

<<functions>>=
def drive_file_size_limit(run):
    """Let the answer log meet a size limit, then lift the limit."""

    limit = max(
        2 * run.store.stat().st_size,
        int(ANSWERS_PER_SECOND * LOG_LINE_BYTES * run.settings.seconds / 4),
    )
    run.restart_server(0, kill=False, file_size_limit=limit)
    time.sleep(max(0.0, run.deadline - time.monotonic()) / 2)
    with run.guard:
        refused = list(run.refused)
    if not refused:
        run.problems.append(f"the log never met the limit of {limit} bytes")
    run.notes.append(
        f"{len(refused)} answers refused at the limit of {limit} bytes,"
        f" with status {sorted(set(refused))}"
    )
    run.restart_server(0, kill=False)
    time.sleep(max(0.0, run.deadline - time.monotonic()))
<<constants>>=
LOG_LINE_BYTES = 100
@

\subsection{A room answering at once}

A class that has just been shown a QR code does not answer forty times a second, it
answers all at once, and the server has one thread and one file descriptor per
connection.  So this scenario connects first, waits until the server's threads are all
waiting on their sockets, and then sends every request in one go.  Connecting first
leaves the queue of waiting connections out of it, which has its own test in the
suite; what is tested here is the handling of the answers.  A round is 200 answers,
which takes a real server less than a second, and it is the only scenario with no
process competing for the store.

<<run methods>>=
def burst(self, count=BURST_ANSWERS):
    """Send `count` answers at the same moment and note the replies."""

    connections = []
    try:
        for _ in range(count):
            connection = socket.create_connection(
                ("127.0.0.1", self.ports[0]), timeout=POST_TIMEOUT
            )
            connections.append((connection, *self._next_answer()))
        time.sleep(BURST_SETTLING)
        for connection, key, value in connections:
            connection.sendall(_answer_request(self.prefixes[key], value))
        for connection, key, value in connections:
            self._read_burst_reply(connection, key, value)
    finally:
        for connection, _, _ in connections:
            connection.close()


def _read_burst_reply(self, connection, key, value):
    reply = b""
    try:
        while part := connection.recv(4096):
            reply += part
    except OSError as error:
        self._note_no_reply(error)
        return
    match = re.match(rb"HTTP/1\.\d (\d+)", reply)
    if match:
        self._note_reply(key, value, int(match.group(1)))
    else:
        self._note_no_reply(ConnectionError("no status line"))
<<functions>>=
def _answer_request(prefix, value):
    body = urllib.parse.urlencode({"value": value})
    return (
        f"POST /{prefix} HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: stress\r\nConnection: close\r\n"
        f"Content-Type: {FORM_TYPE}\r\nContent-Length: {len(body)}\r\n\r\n"
        f"{body}"
    ).encode("ascii")


def drive_bursts(run):
    """Send rounds of simultaneous answers."""

    rounds = 0
    while run.running():
        run.burst()
        rounds += 1
        time.sleep(BURST_PAUSE)
    run.notes.append(f"{rounds} rounds of {BURST_ANSWERS} answers at once")
<<constants>>=
BURST_ANSWERS = 200
BURST_SETTLING = 0.5
BURST_PAUSE = 1.0
