\section{The verdict}
\label{sec:verdict}

A lost answer is the point of the program, but it is not the only thing that should
fail it.  An answer stored twice, a page that returned an error, a build that crashed
or a server that would not stop are all things that must not happen during a lecture.
And a run that accepted no answers at all has tested nothing, which is the one way
this program could otherwise report success without having looked.

A value stored but never accepted is allowed only as far as answers went unanswered:
the request may have been stored before the server died.  More than that means answers
appeared out of nowhere, or the server refused an answer it had kept, which would make
a student's second try count twice.

The server's log is scanned for what it logged as errors.  Those go in the report as
notes and fail nothing by themselves, since the log also records harmless things such
as a client that went away, and what a server has to say about a fault we caused
ourselves.

<<run methods>>=
def judge(self, rows):
    """Print what happened in this run and return its outcome."""

    extra = sum(row.extra for row in rows)
    accepted = sum(len(values) for values in self.accepted.values())
    self.elapsed = self.elapsed or time.monotonic() - self.started_at
    <<note what should not have happened>>
    outcome = Outcome(
        self.name,
        self.elapsed,
        self.builds,
        accepted,
        sum(row.stored for row in rows),
        sum(row.lost for row in rows),
        len(self.refused),
        len(self.no_reply),
        len(self.problems),
    )
    self._print_report(rows)
    return outcome


def _print_report(self, rows):
    print(
        f"=== {self.name}: {self.elapsed:.0f} s, {self.builds} builds,"
        f" {self.killed_builds} killed"
    )
    for row in rows:
        print(f"   {row}")
    print(
        f"   log: {self.malformed_lines} malformed lines, torn tail"
        f" {self.torn_bytes} bytes"
    )
    for note in self.notes[:NOTES_SHOWN]:
        print(f"   note: {note}")
    for error in self._server_errors()[:NOTES_SHOWN]:
        print(f"   server said: {error[:200]}")
    for problem in self.problems:
        print(f"   PROBLEM: {problem}")


def _server_errors(self):
    text = (self.directory / "server.log").read_text(errors="replace")
    return sorted(set(SERVER_ERROR.findall(text)))
