\section{The program}
\label{sec:main}

The working directory has to be on a real disk.  On [[tmpfs]] a sync returns at once,
and the sync is what the design was careful to keep outside the lock, so the threads
would overlap in a different way from a lecture.  A rename there also has no journal
behind it.  A run on [[tmpfs]] would thus test another system than the one a lecturer
has, and pass for the wrong reason.  [[/var/tmp]] is the default, as it is meant for
temporary files and usually lies on a disk, unlike [[/tmp]] on many systems, and
[[TMPDIR]] plays no part, because on the machines this runs on it often points at
memory.  Where the system does not tell, the run goes on and says so.

<<functions>>=
def filesystem_type(path):
    """Return the type of the file system holding `path`, or None if unknown."""

    try:
        mounts = Path("/proc/self/mounts").read_text().splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return None
    target = str(path.resolve())
    best, kind = None, None
    for line in mounts:
        _, mount, fstype, *_ = line.split()
        mount = mount.encode().decode("unicode_escape")
        inside = target == mount or target.startswith(mount.rstrip("/") + "/")
        if inside and (best is None or len(mount) >= len(best)):
            best, kind = mount, fstype
    return kind


def check_real_disk(path):
    """Refuse a directory in memory, and report what `path` is."""

    if not path.is_dir():
        raise HarnessError(f"{path} is not a directory")
    kind = filesystem_type(path)
    if kind in MEMORY_FILESYSTEMS:
        raise HarnessError(
            f"{path} is on {kind}: a sync does nothing there, so this would"
            " test another system; use --dir with a directory on a disk"
        )
    return kind or "unknown file system"
<<constants>>=
DEFAULT_DIRECTORY = "/var/tmp"
MEMORY_FILESYSTEMS = frozenset({"tmpfs", "ramfs"})
@

The old Mentipy lives in a virtual environment of its own in the scratch directory, so
it goes away with the rest and cannot end up in the project's environment.  The
package manager keeps no cache either, which costs a few megabytes of download per run
and leaves nothing behind.  With no network, the scenario can be left out, or
[[--old-python]] can point at an environment made earlier.  We only build the
environment if [[old-builds]] was asked for.

A test of the wrong code is the quietest failure this program can have, so before
anything runs each Python has to show which Mentipy it imports.  The old one has to
be 0.17, and the new one has to come from the source directory: if that directory has
not been tangled, [[import mentipy]] would quietly find the installed package.

The old Python's path is made absolute, since the builds run in the scenario's
directory, but its links are not followed: the link to the system's Python is what
makes it the Python of that environment.

<<functions>>=
def install_old_mentipy(directory):
    """Install the old Mentipy from PyPI in a new environment at `directory`."""

    print(f"installing Mentipy {OLD_MENTIPY} from PyPI ...")
    venv.EnvBuilder(with_pip=True).create(directory)
    python = directory / "bin" / "python"
    done = subprocess.run(
        [
            python,
            "-m",
            "pip",
            "install",
            "--quiet",
            "--no-input",
            "--no-cache-dir",
            "--disable-pip-version-check",
            f"mentipy=={OLD_MENTIPY}",
        ],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
    )
    if done.returncode:
        raise HarnessError(
            f"cannot install Mentipy {OLD_MENTIPY}: {done.stderr.strip()[-500:]}"
            "\nleave out old-builds, or pass --old-python"
        )
    return python


def mentipy_identity(python, environment, directory):
    """Return the version and file of the Mentipy that `python` imports.

    It runs in `directory`, since the current directory is on the path of
    `python -c`.
    """

    done = subprocess.run(
        [python, "-c", IDENTITY_CODE],
        env=environment,
        cwd=directory,
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
    )
    if done.returncode:
        raise HarnessError(f"{python} cannot import mentipy: {done.stderr}")
    version, _, file = done.stdout.strip().partition(" ")
    return version, Path(file)


def prepare_settings(arguments, scratch):
    """Find both Mentipys, verify them, and return the settings of the run."""

    source = arguments.source.resolve()
    config = scratch / "no-config.json"
    environment = mentipy_environment(config, source)
    _, file = mentipy_identity(sys.executable, environment, scratch)
    if source not in file.resolve().parents:
        raise HarnessError(
            f"import mentipy found {file}, not the source in {source}:"
            " has it been tangled?"
        )
    print(f"testing the Mentipy at {file.parent}")
    old_python = arguments.old_python
    if "old-builds" in arguments.scenarios:
        if old_python is None:
            old_python = install_old_mentipy(scratch / "old-mentipy")
        old_python = Path(os.path.abspath(old_python))
        version, _ = mentipy_identity(
            old_python, mentipy_environment(config), scratch
        )
        if version != OLD_MENTIPY:
            raise HarnessError(
                f"{old_python} has Mentipy {version}, not {OLD_MENTIPY}"
            )
        print(f"building old-builds with Mentipy {version}")
    return Settings(arguments.seconds, source, old_python)
